1. THE FOREST — THE CANONICAL OPERATING GRAMMAR

Define a Pantheon semantic object as

S = (intent, actors, context, observations, uncertainties, invariants, operators, permissions, state, provenance, desired_effect, evidence_standard).

An operator transforms this object to a task-specific representation or action proposal:

T_domain(S, tools, environment) -> (artifact, proposed_action, tests, delta, ledger_event)

Subject to:

• Invariants survive meaning translation and reorganization; transformations that lose negation, temporal order, consent, agent identity, or uncertainty must fail or be explicitly labeled lossy.
• External effects require permission: intent is not a substitute for scoped authorization; a real person’s refusal and revocation govern subsequent coordination.
• Truth states remain separate: OBSERVED (sourced evidence), EXECUTED (locally tested action), INFERRED (reasoned conclusion), SPECIFIED (designed but not run), HYPOTHETICAL (unvalidated projection), UNKNOWN (unresolved).
• Global/world state is distinct from viewpoint: lens changes must not rewrite the event ledger.
• Outputs terminate in reality: a specification should eventually yield a test, a working artifact, an explicit rejection, or a named unresolved dependency.
• Reintegration retains failure as well as success: no cherry-picked best tours.

Core loop

ORIENT → RETRIEVE → RECONSTRUCT → CHOOSE LENS → PRESERVE INVARIANTS → FORM SEMANTIC IR → COMPILE → GOVERN → EXECUTE BOUNDED TASK → VERIFY → APPEND EVIDENCE → REINTEGRATE

Nested inside that loop: Mirror (contrast and alternative explanations), Hound (adversarial inspection), Indigo Hammer (break the attractive but fragile inference), Kintsugi (repair while retaining the learned failure), Golden Thread (continuity and provenance), and Message to Garcia (initiative directed toward an authorized goal).

